# C and C++

Use for C or C++ only after build files, compiler settings, includes, syntax, or neighboring sources establish the language. A `.h` extension alone is insufficient.

- Follow the repository's selected C or C++ standard and style; use newer features only when its compiler targets allow them.
- For C++, prefer RAII, value semantics, `const`, and `constexpr`; use `auto` when it improves readability. Avoid raw owning `new` and `delete`; choose ownership-aware smart pointers only when dynamic ownership is needed.
- Do not apply C++ ownership or language rules to C. In C, make allocation ownership and cleanup paths explicit.
- Use the configured formatter, static analyzer, build system, and test framework. Typical choices include `clang-format`, `clang-tidy`, CMake, GoogleTest, or Catch2.
- Preserve repository warning policy. Use sanitizers in supported local or CI configurations; do not force `-Werror` or unsupported sanitizers onto consumers.
